rience making, documenting and defending architecture trade-offs for embedded systems</p></li><li><p>A quality mindset: hands-on test experience, CI and coverage as everyday tools, and code reviews with constructive feedback.</p></li><li><p>Critical, analytical, systematic thinking: you reason from first principles, make your assumptions explicit and challenge existing paradigms</p></li></ul><p><strong>Strongly preferred</strong></p><ul><li><p>Deep embedded experience incl. async Rust on embedded targets, down to the executor: futures, wakers, pinning</p></li><li><p>Architecture of multicore and multi-controller systems: the partition of work, data and timing across cores and controllers</p></li><li><p>Hands-on debugging and tracing on ARM and RISC-V</p></li><li><p>Knowledge of various communication protocols and stacks: CAN, LIN, BLE, LTE modems</p></li><li><p>Basic hardware skills: reading schematics, working with an oscilloscope, board bring-up, debugging</p></li></ul><p><strong>Nice to have</strong></p><ul><li><p>Formal methods for embedded software: model checking, or a proof of a scheduling or memory property</p></li><li><p>A background in safety-critical (IEC 61508, ISO 26262, …) and battery systems</p></li><li><p>Comfortable implementing numerical algorithms (filtering, state estimation) on constrained hardware</p></li><li><p>Experience mentoring other engineers</p></li></ul><p>You don't tick every box? Apply anyway. No one matches a job description 100%. If this role excites you and you see yourself growing into it, we want to hear from you.</p><h2>What We Offer</h2><ul><li><p>Work on a product with purpose that redefines the state of art: our product is a key part to enable competitive battery energy storage.</p></li><li><p>Work directly with the founding team on decisions that shape the company. The team is small, so your ideas will matter. A lot.</p></li><li><p>Challenging engineering in an industrial, but research-near environment. We bring research into the industry.</p></li><li><p>A modern stack and methods: Rust, MBSE, everything as code, and the best tools and techniques for the job, chosen on merit.</p></li><li><p>To be both supported and challenged: room to grow, and the expectation that you will.</p></li></ul><h2>You might be a fit if</h2><ul><li><p>You hold yourself to a high standard and don’t settle for "good enough."</p></li><li><p>You want to have a big say in what the Anori BMS looks like and how it works</p></li><li><p>You are eager to try out new tools and enjoy incorporating AI into your work to make your life easier and more productive</p></li><li><p>You are excited about startups and all the opportunities and uncertainties that come with it</p></li><li><p>You want to be an active part in accelerating the energy transition in Europe</p></li><li><p>You are comfortable with English as a working language</p></li><li><p>You are happy to be in the office most of the time because for fast decisions we need to be in the same room. We expect you to be in the office at least 3 days / week</p></li></ul><h2>Interview Process</h2><p><strong>1. First Call </strong>A short chat with one of our founders. We'll get to know your background and answer any questions you have about Anori Tech.<em> 30 min</em></p><p><strong>2. Deep Dive with Case Study (on-site) </strong>A meeting with Stefan, our CTO. We want to understand how you work and talk through your past projects. The meeting also includes a small case study, and we will send you some context information for it beforehand. <em>2 - 3 hours</em></p><p><strong>3. Meet the Team (on-site)</strong>Time to meet your future colleagues! In this meeting we get to know each other. This is also the time to discuss final details like salary. <em>1 - 2 hours</em></p><p>🏬 The on-site meetings will be at our lab at the Bahrenfeld Startup Lab: Luruper Hauptstraße 1, 22547 Altona</p><p>You also want to become an Anori? We cannot wait to meet you. Go and click the apply button.